[P11] [E] Peoples Pension Policy Info.md (score 0.300)
either a bit at a time or in one lump sum. For every £1 you take as tax-free cash, £3 will be moved into a flexi-access drawdown account. Then each time you take money out of the flexi-access drawdown account, you may pay tax on the full amount of each lump sum. It's added to your taxable income and, depending on your tax status, parts of it may be liable for higher rates of tax.
The second option is through an 'uncrystallised funds pension lump sum' (UFPLS). This involves spreading your tax-free cash across all withdrawals. Each time you take money from your pension pot, 25% of it is tax free. The other 75% is added to your taxable income. Depending on your tax status, parts of it may be liable for higher rates of tax.
People's Partnership doesn't offer an option to purchase an annuity, which offers a guaranteed income, therefore there are no guarantees to the amount you may be able to take as income from your savings. But you can use your money to buy an annuity, or to access any of the other options listed above, from another provider.
There are lots of things you should consider before deciding on when and how to access your pension savings. You can read more about these considerations and your retirement options with The People's Pension at www.thepeoplespension.co.uk/your-retirement-options ,
