#### How to compare
Method: ValueMatch
Tolerance: Plan values must match the provider statement to the penny where the same as-at date is used; otherwise flag the date gap, not the value.
- Guard: Fund value and transfer value are different figures; a report quoting one against a statement quoting the other is not automatically a mismatch.
- Guard: Values move between the provider statement date and the report date — check both dates before flagging.
- Guard: A protection plan has no fund value to reconcile — compare sum assured and premium instead.
- Guard: Membership is part of consistency, and it is the part a value comparison cannot reach. Every arrangement the report tabulates must appear in the fact find's own pension schedule, and every arrangement the fact find records must be accounted for in the report. A plan present in one document and absent from the other is a finding in its own right — list the plans on each side by provider before comparing any figure.
- Establish what each figure is ON before calling two of them contradictory. Two values on different bases — one including a charge, a tax or a fee the other excludes, one at a different date, age or term — are not two answers to one question. Name both bases in "analysis". A basis the report never states is one finding about disclosure, not one finding per row of the table.
- And the reverse: where a comparison between an existing option and a recommended one shows no difference, ask what assumption produced that. A charge, a rate or a term applied to one side and not the other makes two unlike things look alike. Say which assumptions were applied to each side, or name what you could not establish in "missingInputs".

