eral Expenses £20,616), which equals £2,000.08 per month.","discrepancies":"[1] Total monthly income: Report derives £3,486.67 net monthly; Fact Find shows £1,430.00 net monthly. Difference: £2,056.67 (144% higher in report). [2] C1 employment income: Report states £300/week = £1,300/month derived; Fact Find [P21] shows Basic Income £1,200.00 net monthly. Difference: £100/month (8.3% variance). [3] C1 State Pension: Report derives £230/week = £1,000/month; Fact Find [P21] shows £230.00 net monthly. Difference: £770/month (report treats weekly as monthly equivalent at 4.33x multiplier, Fact Find treats as monthly). [4] Total monthly expenditure: Report states £1,700; Fact Find [P1] shows £1,718.00. Difference: £18 (1% variance, within rounding). [5] Retirement expenditure: Report states \"approximately £1,700\" per month in narrative [P17]; planning documents [P2], [P5], [P6] show first-year regular expenditure of £24,001 annually = £2,000.08 monthly. Difference: £300.08/month (17.6% variance). [6] State Pension annualised: Report states £11,973 per annum [P17]; £230/week × 52 = £11,960 per annum. Difference: £13 (0.1% variance, rounding). [7] Monthly surplus: Report derives £1,786.67 (£3,486.67 - £1,700); Fact Find shows -£288.00 (£1,430.00 - £1,718.00). Difference: £2,074.67 (report shows surplus, Fact Find shows deficit).","comparisonPerformed":true,"missingInputs":[],"analysis":"The comparison reveals material inconsistencies between the report's income and expenditure figures and the supporting evidence. \n\n**Income discrepancies:** The report's derived total monthly income of £3,486.67 contradicts the Fact Find's stated total of £1,430.00. The critical error is in the State Pension conversion: the report treats £230 per week as £1,000 per month (multiplying by 4.33), but the Fact Find [P21] records State Pension as £230.00 per month, not weekly. This is a basis mismatch — the report appears to have misinterpreted the frequency. The C1 employment income also shows a £100/month variance (£1,300 derived vs £1,200 stated in Fact Find), suggesting the report may have used a different weekly-to-monthly conversion (£300/week × 52/12 = £1,300, whereas the Fact Find records £1,200 net monthly as the actual stated figure). The report includes C2's £1,100/month employment income in the total, which is correct, but the State Pension treatment is fundamentally wrong.\n\n**Expenditure discrepancies:** The report states household expenditure of £1,700/month, which is close to the Fact Find's £1,718.00 (1% variance, acceptable rounding). However, the planning documents [P2], [P5], [P6] show first-year regular expenditure of £24,001 annually (£2,000.08/month), which is £300/month higher than the report's stated £1,700. The narrative in [P17] states \"In retirement, you expect your annual expenditure to be approximately £1,700\" — this appears to be a frequency error in the report itself, as £1,700 annually would be £141.67/month, which is implausibly low. The planning documents treat this as £20,616 in general expenses plus £3,385 tax/NI = £24,001 annually, suggesting the report's £1,700 figure is intended as monthly but the planning model uses a different (higher) figure.\n\n**Surplus/deficit contradiction:** The report derives a monthly surplus of £1,786.67, implying the household is in a strong financial position. The Fact Find shows a monthly deficit of -£288.00, indicating expenditure exceeds income. This is a material contradiction that affects affordability assessment. The discrepancy arises from the compounded errors in income (overstated by £2,056.67) and the relatively minor expenditure variance (£18).\n\n**Basis issues:** The State Pension is the primary source of error. The report converts £230/week to £1,000/month using a 4.33 multiplier (52 weeks ÷ 12 months), which is arithmetically correct for annualising a weekly figure. However, the Fact Find records State Pension as £230.00 monthly, not weekly. This suggests either: (a) the Fact Find is recording the weekly amount in a monthly field, or (b) the report misread the frequency. Given that [P17] explicitly states \"You receive state pension income of £11,973 per annum\" and £230/week × 52 = £11,960 (matching the annualised figure), the report's interpretation of £230 as weekly is supported by the narrative. However, the Fact Find [P21] labels this as \"Monthly\" frequency with £230.00 amount, creating an internal inconsistency in the source documents themselves.\n\n**Planning model alignment:** The planning documents [P2], [P5], [P6] show expenditure of £24,001 annually in the first year, which does not reconcile with the report's stated £1,700/month (£20,400 annually). The planning model includes Tax & NI of £3,385, whereas the report's affordability assessment does not explicitly account for this in the stated £1,700 figure. This suggests the report's £1,700 may be net of tax, while the planning model's £24,001 is gross of tax.\n\n**Conclusion on comparison:** The comparison can be performed, but it reveals that the report's income figures are materially inconsistent with the Fact Find. The State Pension frequency interpretation is the primary driver of the discrepancy. The expenditure figures are closer but still show material variance when compared to the planning model. The monthly surplus/deficit contradiction is a direct consequence of the income overstatement.","citations":[{"passageId":"P1","source":"Fact Find (Test 1).md","category":"B","quote":"Total Net Monthly Income £ 1,430.00 Total Monthly Expenditure £ 1,718.00 Total Monthly Disposable Income £ -288.00","cells":null},{"passageId":"P21","source":"Fact Find (Test 1).md","category":"B","quote":"Basic Income (Employed) £ 1,200.00 net monthly; State Pension £ 230.00 net monthly","cells":null},{"passageId":"P17","source":"Suitability Report Test 1.md","category":"I","quote":"You receive £300 per week as an HGV driver. You receive £20 per week helping out the sea cadets. Kim 