nsion. The report tabulates five arrangements: People's Pension, Scottish Widows, Aviva, Zurich, and Standard Life. Three of the five do not appear in the Fact Find's pension section, though they appear elsewhere in the case file (e.g., [P20] Standard Life, [P21] policy research). This suggests incomplete recording in the Fact Find's structured pension section.\n\nOn tax status: The Fact Find records \"Highest rate of income tax paid: 0%\". The report's cost comparison assumes marginal-rate taxation (e.g., £170 net of £200 encashment implies 15% tax). This is a material assumption not supported by the Fact Find.\n\nOn expenditure frequency: The cashflow input list states \"In retirement, you expect your annual expenditure to be approximately £1,700\" — but £1,700 is treated as monthly everywhere else in the report and Fact Find. This is a presentation inconsistency that could cause confusion.\n\nAge reconciliation: The Fact Find records Date of Birth 07/06/1956. At the fact-find date (19/09/2025) this gives age 69; at the report date (14/10/2025) this also gives age 69. The report states age 69, which is consistent with the derived DOB. The extraction's own report flags F1.4 as a Fact Find internal contradiction (age 70 stated alongside DOB 07/06/1956), but the report's assertion of 69 is correct for the report date.\n\nThe guard on age (tolerance of 1 year where fact find predates report) does not apply here — the Fact Find predates the report by 25 days, and the derived age is consistent. The guard on spouse details (do not treat non-advised client's details as mismatch) applies to Kim's age (65 corroborated by [P1]), but not to her income, which is a household financial fact.\n\nThe guard on basis (establish what each figure is on before calling two contradictory) applies to the State Pension discrepancy: the report treats it as weekly, the Fact Find as monthly. These are different bases and the discrepancy is material. It also applies to income totals: the report includes Kim's employment and Sea Cadets income; the Fact Find does not. The basis is unclear — is Kim's income excluded from the Fact Find's income table, or was it not disclosed?\n\nThe guard on assumptions (where a comparison shows no difference, ask what assumption produced it) does not apply here — the comparisons show material differences, not false matches.","citations":[{"passageId":"P2","source":"Fact Find (Test 1).md","category":"B","quote":"Gender: Male, Marital Status: Married","cells":null},{"passageId":"P1","source":"Fact Find (Test 1).md","category":"B","quote":"07/05/1960, 65, Yes","cells":null},{"passageId":"P13","source":"Suitability Report Test 1.md","category":"I","quote":"You are currently aged 69 and married to Kim, aged 65. You are both in good health and have three grown-up children, all of whom are financially independent. You work full-time as an HGV driver through an agency, typically earning around £300 per week net, with additional income of £20 per week from your volunteer role with the Sea Cadets. Kim earns approximately £1,100 per month net.","cells":null},{"passageId":"P15","source":"Fact Find (Test 1).md","category":"B","quote":"Net Basic Monthly Income: £1,200.00","cells":null},{"passageId":"P17","source":"Fact Find (Test 1).md","category":"B","quote":"Total: £6,000.00","cells":["Cash","Savings","GBP","6,000.00","22/09/2025"]},{"passageId":"P13","source":"Suitability Report Test 1.md","category":"I","quote":"You are in receipt of your full State Pension of £230 per week, and Kim will receive her full State Pension of around £230 per week when she reaches State Pension age.","cells":null},{"passageId":"P17","source":"Fact Find (Test 1).md","category":"B","quote":"State Pension: £230.00, frequency Monthly","cells":["State Pension","State Pension","Monthly","£230.00"]},{"passageId":"P1","source":"Fact Find (Test 1).md","category":"B","quote":"Dependent?: Living with, 07/05/1960, 65, Yes","cells":null},{"passageId":"P13","source":"Su