urplus / Deficit\" of 0 for all years until 2056, when deficits begin (£2,930 in 2056, £2,876 in 2057, £3,014 in 2058). The planning document states: \"A Required withdrawal (Investments) is made from available liquid assets when there is a shortfall in a year (where expenditure is greater than income).\" The surplus income percentage saved is 0%, meaning no surplus is retained beyond what is needed.","discrepancies":"1. The report asserts the emergency fund is \"sufficient\" and \"retained\" without quantifying what level of emergency reserve is adequate or what the planning assumptions show about its depletion. 2. The report does not disclose that the £6,000 emergency fund is scheduled to be drawn down gradually from £6,000 (2025) to £4,773 (2056) and then to £0 by 2058 in the planning projections. 3. The report does not explain that the emergency fund is the \"first liquid asset to be drawn from if there is a shortfall\" per the planning assumptions, meaning it is not ring-fenced but is part of the general liquidity pool used to meet any cashflow deficit. 4. The report states the client is \"comfortable with the concept as you have liquid cash should you require instance access funds\" but does not disclose that by age 102/98 (2058), the planning shows liquid assets at £0, leaving no emergency reserves available. 5. The report does not address the timing of when the emergency fund becomes depleted relative to the client's life expectancy or foreseeable needs.","comparisonPerformed":true,"missingInputs":[],"analysis":"The comparison can be made because both the report and the planning documents are present. The report asserts that the emergency fund of £6,000 is \"sufficient\" and that the client \"retain[s] sufficient liquid capital to cover any unexpected emergencies.\" However, the intelliflo planning projections show that this £6,000 is not a separate, protected reserve but is instead part of the general liquid asset pool that is drawn down to meet cashflow shortfalls. The planning assumptions explicitly state that the surplus account (which holds the £6,000 cash) is \"the first liquid asset to be drawn from if there is a shortfall.\" The Proposed scenario (reflecting the recommended position) shows liquid assets declining from £6,000 to £0 by age 102/98 (year 2058). While the report does not explicitly state that the emergency fund will be depleted, the planning documents show that it will be. The report's assertion that the client \"retain[s] sufficient liquid capital\" is not supported by the planning projections, which show the liquid assets (including the emergency fund) being systematically drawn down and reaching zero. The guard states: \"A cashflow that schedules the emergency fund to be spent has not retained it, whatever the report says about retaining liquid capital. Read the liquidation order in the scenario before accepting an assertion of sufficiency.\" The planning documents show the emergency fund is part of the liquidation order and will be drawn. The report does not disclose this depletion or address whether £6,000 is adequate as an emergency reserve given that it will be consumed by age 102/98. The report also does not quantify what level of emergency reserve is considered sufficient or explain the basis for the client's confirmation that £6,000 is adequate. The client's stated comfort with \"liquid cash should you require instance access funds\" is not supported by a disclosure that this cash will be depleted within the planning horizon. The initial commitment of £110,185 (transfer of existing pensions) is a one-time transfer, not an ongoing contribution, so affordability of the transfer itself is not the issue; rather, the issue is whether the emergency fund provision is adequate and whether it will remain available when needed.","citations":[{"passageId":"P1","source":"intelliflo planning Baseline scenario.md","category":"F","quote":"£6,000","cells":null},{"passageId":"P2","source":"intelliflo plann