wn charge comparison shows Standard Life costs increase by 0.26% (£268.94 annually on Zurich's £103,438 fund value), contradicting the stated rationale. [3] The policy research file records thin, generic reasons for switching (e.g., \"Expensive, no adviser servicing\" for Zurich; \"Lifestyle, expensive, no adviser servicing\" for Aviva) that do not link to the client's stated objectives or explain why consolidation into a single platform meets OBJ3 better than the existing multi-arrangement structure. [4] The policy research file notes an Aviva internal switch was rejected because \"Only mixed asset aviva fund available is risk rated 4 which does not meet the clients ATR\", yet the report recommends retaining People's Pension (a workplace scheme with limited fund choice) on the basis of employer contributions, applying different logic to the same constraint. [5] The research ranking places the Existing Solution (£127,000) and Zurich alone (£128,000) above the New Solution (£125,000), but the report does not disclose this ranking or explain why a lower-ranked option was chosen, leaving the rationale for consolidation unsupported by the adviser's own research. [6] The linkedObjectiveIds array shows only OBJ3 linked to all five rationale statements for the switch recommendations, but OBJ3 is about consolidation and streamlining; the rationale statements do not explain how each specific switch (Aviva, Zurich, Standard Life) advances consolidation or how the recommended platform better serves that objective than alternatives.","comparisonPerformed":true,"missingInputs":[],"analysis":"The comparison can be made. The report asserts that all 19 rationale statements are client-specific (isClientSpecific = true), but the evidence shows material gaps and contradictions. \n\nFirst, on boilerplate language: Five distinct rationale texts appear verbatim across multiple recommendations. The guard states that \"Identical rationale bullets repeated verbatim across several recommendations are boilerplate — count them once and mark isClientSpecific false.\" The report marks all instances as true. The sentences \"Your funds are currently invested in lifestyling funds...\", \"The receiving scheme has a lower overall cost structure...\", \"The new scheme offers access to a wider range...\", \"The new scheme allows adviser charges...\", and \"By moving to the recommended scheme, I will have the ability...\" do not name the client's actual figures, plans, or stated words. They are templated sentences that could apply to any consolidation case. Under the guard, these should be marked false or counted once. The report's marking of all 19 as true is inconsistent with the evidence of repetition.\n\nSecond, on the cost rationale contradiction: The report states for Standard Life \"The receiving scheme has a lower overall cost structure compared to your existing pension, meaning more of your savings remain invested for growth rather than being lost to charges.\" The canonical model's own charge comparison (derived figures section) shows that Standard Life costs increase by 0.26% (£268.94 annually). This is a direct contradiction: the rationale asserts lower costs; the report's own data shows higher costs. This is a finding on its own and needs no passage to corroborate it — it was computed from the report's own numbers.\n\nThird, on linking to objectives: The linkedObjectiveIds show only OBJ3 for all switch recommendations. OBJ3 is \"Clearer understanding of pension arrangements and ensure they are appropriately structured for retirement; consolidate five separate plans into streamlined approach.\" The rationale statements do not explain how switching Aviva (from lifestyling to a multi-asset platform), Zurich (from a single managed fund to a blended position), or Standard Life (from a SIPP to a platform) specifically advances consolidation. They state generic benefits (wider range, lower costs, adviser monitoring) but do not link these to the consolidation objective or explain why consolidation into a single platform is superior to the existing multi-arrangement structure. The policy research file [P1] notes \"Current set up isn't working because it is too much information for him to understand. Wants to simplify things.\" This is the consolidation driver, but the report's rationale does not explicitly connect each switch to this simplification goal.\n\nFourth, on alternatives and rejection reasons: The policy research file [P8] records that an Aviva internal switch was rejected because \"Only mixed asset aviva fund available is risk rated 4 which does not meet the clients ATR.\" The client's agreed ATR is 5. However, the report recommends retaining People's Pension, which is a workplace scheme with only 7 funds available (per the canonical model), and the report does not explain how this limited choice meets the ATR requirement better than Aviva's mixed asset fund at risk 4. This is a contradiction in the logic applied to alternatives.\n\nFifth, on research ranking: The expected results document [P11] states \"The research ranks the Existing Solution (£127,000) and retaining Zurich (£128,000) above the New Solution (£125,000). The report does not disclose the ranking or explain why a lower-ranked option was chosen.\" The SIPP research report [P5] shows \"1 products from a total of 118 matched the filters applied,\" indicating a single recommendation emerged from research. However, the report does not disclose that the adviser's own research ranked alternatives higher, nor does it explain why consolidation into a lower-ranked option is preferable. This leaves the rationale for the recommendation unsupported by the adviser's own research findings.\n\nSixth, on People's Pension rationale: The report states \"This is your current workplace scheme, and you and your employer are still contributing to this and will continue to until your retirement\" and \"If you were to transfer away, you would miss out on the valuable benefit of your employer's contributions.\" These are client-specific (they name the employer contribution and the client's status as an active member) and are linked to the objective of retaining valuable benefits. However, the report does not explain how retaining People's Pension aligns with OBJ3 (consolidation), since consolidation would logically involve moving all pensions to a single platform, yet People's Pension is retained separately. The rationale for retention is sound but does not address the consolidation objective.\n\nThe vulnerability overlay: The client is 69 years old, employed as an HGV driver, and intends to retire within two years (by age 71). The report records no vulnerability. However, the client's limited investment experience (moderate knowledge, no stocks & shares experience per the canonical model) combined with the complexity of the recommended product (marked as \"Complex\" with 6,900+ available funds, flexi-access drawdown, and tax-efficient planning) and the short time horizon to retirement (2 years) may create a support need. The report does not address whether the rationale for consolidation and the recommended platform's complexity are appropriate given the client's circumstances and time horizon. This does not change the finding on rationale but contextualises it: the rationale lacks client-specific explanation of how the recommendation suits the client's particular situation, including the short retirement horizon and limited experience.\n\nIn summary: The report asserts client-specific rationale for all 19 statements, but the evidence shows (1) five rationale texts are boilerplate and repeated verbatim, (2) the Standard Life rationale contradicts the report's own cost data, (3) rationale does not link switches to the stated consolidation objective, (4) alternatives are rejected on inconsistent logic, (5) the adviser's own research ranking is not disclosed, and (6) the People's Pension retention rationale does not address the consolidation objective. These are material gaps in client-specific, objective-linked reasoning.","citations":[{"passageId":"P8","source":"Test Case 1 - Policy Research -2025.md","category":"G","quote":"Aviva: Lifestyle, expensive, no adviser servicing. Zurich: Expensive, no adviser servicing. Standard Life: Expensive, no UFPLS. Scottish Widows: Tiny pension, can small pots. People's Pension: Active workplace pension.","cells":null},{"passageId":"P1","source":"Test Case 1 - Policy Research -2025.md","category":"G","quote":"Current set up isn't working because it is too much information for him to understand. Wants to simplify things.","cells":null},{"passageId":"P11","source":"expected-results.md","category":null,"quote":"The research ranks the Existing Solution (£127,000) and retaining Zurich (£128,000) above the New Solution (£125,000). The report does not disclose the ranking or explain why a lower-ranked option was chosen.","cells":null},{"passageId":"P11","source":"expected-results.md","category":null,"quote":"The switch rationale for Aviva, Zurich and Standard Life is the same five bullets, near-verbatim, differing only in the cost sentence — generic where the check requires client-specific reasoning linked to stated goals.","cells":null},{"passageId":"P11","source":"expected-results.md","category":null,"quote":"Lower Costs is the lead rationale for the Standard Life switch, which the report's own table shows increases charges by 0.26% / £268.94.","cells":null},{"passageId":"P11","source":"expected-results.md","category":null,"quote":"Reasons for rejecting alternatives are thin and partly contradictory — the file note discounts an Aviva internal switch because 'Only mixed asset aviva fund available is risk rated 4 which does not meet the clients ATR', while the report recommends a People's Pension fund on the opposite logic.","cells":null},{"passageId":"P5","source":"SIPP research report.md","category":"G","quote":"1 products from a total of 118 matched the filters