{"groupId":"G7.3","requirement":"Cumulative impact of costs and charges: Consistency. The suitability report must disclose all material charges relevant to the recommendation, including applicable initial, ongoing, product, platform, investment and transaction costs. Where higher costs arise, the report must clearly explain the benefits expected to be received by the client in exchange for those costs and these must be supported by and consistent with the evidence provided. The cumulative impact of costs and charges must be shown and arithmetically sound.","reportSays":"The report asserts an initial adviser charge of £3,305 (3% of current transfer values) and an ongoing annual adviser charge of 1% per annum (stated as \"circa £1,101.85 for the first year\"). It presents a Critical Yield analysis showing Reduction in Yield (RIY) figures: Existing 0.3% across all growth scenarios; Recommended 2.1% (Low), 2.2% (Mid), 2.3% (High). It states Critical Yield requirements of 1.9% (Low), 4.8% (Mid), 7.8% (High) for the recommended solution. The report presents a \"Pension Switch Charges Only Assessment\" table showing annual recurring charges for three existing arrangements (Aviva 0.50%/£18.72, Zurich 0.93%/£961.98, Standard Life 0.18%/£186.19) and impacts of the recommended alternative (-0.06%/-£2.25 for Aviva, -0.49%/-£506.85 for Zurich, +0.26%/+£268.94 for Standard Life). The report states \"The plan itself is 0.08% more expensive than the recommended solution\" in the context of Zurich. It presents projected values at age 75 under three growth scenarios showing switching effects of -£10,400 (9.6%), -£12,000 (9.4%), and -£15,000 (10.0%) respectively. The report justifies the recommendation by stating \"Lower Costs - The receiving scheme has a lower overall cost structure\" and lists benefits including \"Enhanced Retirement Flexibility\", \"Adviser Charging Facilitation\", and \"Ongoing Monitoring and Adjustments\".","fileSays":"The Critical Yield table in [P3] corroborates the RIY figures (0.3% Existing, 2.1%/2.2%/2.3% Recommended) and Critical Yield percentages (1.9%/4.8%/7.8%) and the switching effects (£10,400 9.6%, £12,000 9.4%, £15,000 10.0%). The Pension Switch Charges Only Assessment table in [P13] corroborates the charge figures for Aviva (0.50%, £18.72), Zurich (0.93%, £961.98), and Standard Life (0.18%, £186.19) and the impacts (-0.06%/-£2.25, -0.49%/-£506.85, +0.26%/+£268.94). The illustration in [P9] shows \"How the charges reduce the value of your pension fund\" with a table showing values \"Before charges\", \"After taking product and investment charges\", and \"After taking all charges\", with a final value at age 75 of £115,000 (compared to £129,000 before charges and £126,000 after product/investment charges only). The illustration states that \"All charges could reduce this pension fund from £129,000.00 to £115,000.00 - this is the same as reducing the investment growth after inflation from 2.9% to 0.8%. This is a reduction in the growth rate of 2.1%.\" [P12]. The Key Features document [P17] states that \"The price of some investments includes initial and yearly costs that will have an effect on your investments\" and that \"Depending on how funds take their charges has an impact on your investment growth or income.\" The pension switch report [P21], [P22], [P23] shows projected maturity values for different arrangements at different growth rates. The report narrative in [P14] states benefits including \"Lower Costs\", \"Enhanced Retirement Flexibility\", \"Adviser Charging Facilitation\", and \"Ongoing Monitoring and Adjustments\".","discrepancies":"[1] The report states ongoing adviser charge as \"circa £1,101.85 for the first year\" based on \"current values & expected contributions, minus the initial fee\". However, the calculation appears inconsistent: £110,185 × 1% = £1,101.85 (matching total transfer value), but if calculated on value minus initial fee (£110,185 - £3,305.55 = £106,879.45 × 1% = £1,068.79), the figure should be £1,068.79, not £1,101.85. [2] The report states \"The plan itself is 0.08% more expensive than the recommended solution\" in the Zurich analysis context, but the Pension Switch Charges Only Assessment table [P13] shows Standard Life impact as +0.26% (more expensive), not 0.08%. The charge differential appears to apply to Zurich (where -0.49% is shown), not Standard Life. [3] The Critical Yield table shows projected values at age 75 of £108,000 (Existing Low), £127,000 (Existing Mid), £150,000 (Existing High) versus £97,600 (Recommended Low), £115,000 (Recommended Mid), £135,000 (Recommended High). However, the illustration [P9] shows a final value at age 75 of £115,000 \"After taking all charges\" but the underlying \"Before charges\" value is stated as £129,000, not £127,000 (the mid-growth existing value). This suggests the illustration may be based on different assumptions or a different starting value. [4] The report presents RIY figures of 2.1%, 2.2%, 2.3% for the recommended solution but does not clearly break down which charges (adviser, platform, product, fund) comprise these figures, making it unclear whether all material charges are included in the cumulative impact calculation. [5] The switching effects shown (-£10,400, -£12,000, -£15,000) represent the cost of switching in terms of projected value reduction, but the report does not clearly state whether the initial adviser fee of £3,305 is already deducted from these figures or represents an additional cost.","comparisonPerformed":true,"missingInputs":["Breakdown of RIY components (adviser charge, platform charge, product charge, fund charge) for the recommended solution","Explicit statement of whether the £3,305 initial adviser fee is included in or additional to the switching effect figures","Transaction costs or transfer costs associated with switching","Detailed comparison of all-in costs (adviser + platform + product + fund) for each existing arrangement versus the recommended solution","Basis for the £129,000 'before charges' figure in the illustration and how it relates to the £127,000 mid-growth existing value in the Critical Yield table"],"analysis":"The report presents cumulative cost information through the Critical Yield analysis and the illustration, but several inconsistencies and gaps undermine the clarity and reliability of the cumulative impact disclosure. \n\nFirst, the ongoing adviser charge calculation is arithmetically problematic. The report states the ongoing charge is \"circa £1,101.85 for the first year\" based on \"current values & expected contributions, minus the initial fee\". If the ongoing charge is 1% and is calculated on the total transfer value of £110,185, the result is £1,101.85. However, the report's stated basis (\"minus the initial fee\") would suggest the calculation should be on £106,879.45 (£110,185 - £3,305.55), yielding £1,068.79. The report does not clarify which basis applies, creating ambiguity about the true ongoing cost.\n\nSecond, the statement \"The plan itself is 0.08% more expensive than the recommended solution\" in the Zurich analysis context contradicts the Pension Switch Charges Only Assessment table, which shows Standard Life with a +0.26% impact (more expensive), not 0.08%. The 0.08% figure does not appear in the table at all. This discrepancy suggests either a transcription error or confusion about which arrangement the statement refers to.\n\nThird, there is a potential inconsistency between the Critical Yield table and the illustration regarding the base values. The Critical Yield table shows an Existing mid-growth projected value of £127,000 at age 75, but the illustration shows a \"Before charges\" value of £129,000. The illustration states this is based on \"an example growth rate of 5.0% to which we have then applied price inflation at 2%\", which differs from the 2.95% mid-growth rate used in the Critical Yield table. However, the guard notes that illustration growth rates are prescribed and will differ from cashflow assumptions, so this may not be an inconsistency. The report does not explicitly state the basis for each figure, making it difficult to verify whether they are comparable.\n\nFourth, the RIY figures (2.1%, 2.2%, 2.3% for the recommended solution) are presented without a clear breakdown of their components. The report does not specify which charges are included: adviser charges, platform charges, product charges, fund charges, or all of these. The illustration [P12] states that \"Product and Investment charges could reduce this pension fund from £129,000.00 to £126,000.00 - this is a reduction in the growth rate of 0.4%\" and \"All charges could reduce this pension fund from £129,000.00 to £115,000.00 - this is a reduction in the growth rate of 2.1%\". This suggests the 2.1% RIY figure in the Critical Yield table may represent all charges (adviser + product + investment), but the report does not explicitly confirm this.\n\nFifth, the switching effects (-£10,400, -£12,000, -£15,000) represent the projected value reduction due to switching, but it is unclear whether the initial adviser fee of £3,305 is already deducted from these figures or represents an additional cost on top. The pension switch report [P24] states \"This results in a shortfall in the death benefit immediately after the switch of £3,305.00\", confirming the initial fee reduces the fund value at the point of switching, but the relationship between this immediate impact and the longer-term switching effects in the Critical Yield table is not clearly explained.\n\nSixth, the report does not provide a comprehensive all-in cost comparison. While the Pension Switch Charges Only Assessment table shows annual recurring charges for three existing arrangements and the impact of the recommended alternative, it does not show the total ongoing charges for the recommended solution (adviser + platform + product + fund). The illustration provides some of this information, but the report narrative does not synthesize it into a clear cumulative cost statement.\n\nThe benefits cited to justify the higher costs (or lower costs, as claimed) include \"Enhanced Retirement Flexibility\", \"Adviser Charging Facilitation\", and \"Ongoing Monitoring and Adjustments\". These are supported by the evidence in [P14] and [P16], which describe the flexibility of the Aviva platform and the adviser's ability to monitor and adjust investments. However, the report does not quantify the value of these benefits or explain how they offset any cost differences.\n\nThe guard regarding real-terms versus nominal projections applies here: the Critical Yield table states \"Values shown are in real terms which mean that they have been adjusted to take into account the effect of inflation\" [P1], while the illustration [P12] states \"These projections illustrate what you might get back in 'today's money' which means it takes the effect of price inflation at 2.00% into account\". Both are in real terms, so this is not a basis mismatch.\n\nThe guard regarding prescribed illustration growth rates also applies: the illustration uses a prescribed 2.9% growth rate (based on 5.0% less 2% inflation), while the Critical Yield table uses 0.00%, 2.95%, and 5.88% growth rates. These are different bases and are not directly comparable, which the report acknowledges by presenting them separately.","citations":[{"passageId":"P3","source":"Suitability Report Test 1.md","category":"I","quote":"Reduction in yield: Existing 0.3%, Recommended 2.1% (Low), 2.2% (Mid), 2.3% (High); Critical yield: 1.9% (Low), 4.8% (Mid), 7.8% (High); Effect of switching: £10,400 or 9.6% decrease (Low), £12,000 or 9.4% decrease (Mid), £15,000 or 10.0% decrease (High)","cells":null},{"passageId":"P13","source":"Suitability Report Test 1.md","category":"I","quote":"Aviva 0.50% £18.72 -0.06% -£2.25; Zurich 0.93% £961.98 -0.49% -£506.85; Standard Life 0.18% £186.19 0.26% £268.94","cells":null},{"passageId":"P14","