eport shows RIY figures of 0.3% for existing and 0.6% for new solution at mid growth. The Aviva illustration [P1] and Standard Life illustration [P2] provide product-level projections. The SIPP research report [P14] confirms the agreed risk level of 5 and investment of £110,185. The expected results document [P16] identifies that \"The switch reduces projected maturity value at every growth rate, before advice charges (−1.6% at mid growth) and after them (−9.4%)\" and notes \"The adviser's own ranking places the existing arrangement above the recommendation.\"","discrepancies":"1. Cost direction contradiction: Report asserts \"Aviva plan is 0.24% less expensive\" (RecommendedCheaper) but also states \"Aviva plan is 4.24% more expensive\" in the required outperformance context. These cannot both be true on the same basis.\n2. Projected value mismatch: Report projects £115,000 at mid growth for recommended solution; switch report [P6] shows £125,000 for new solution at mid growth. The report's figure is £10,000 lower than the evidence.\n3. Critical yield discrepancy: Report states 4.8% critical yield; switch report [P6] shows 3.2% critical yield for the new solution at mid growth. The report's figure is 1.6 percentage points higher.\n4. Reduction in yield mismatch: Report extracts RIY values of 0.3%, 2.1%, 0.3%, 2.2%, 0.3%, 2.3% (six values); switch report [P6] shows 0.3% for existing and 0.6% for new solution at mid growth, with 0.3% for existing and 0.6% for new at low and high growth rates. The report's extracted RIY values do not align with the switch report's stated RIY figures.\n5. Effect of switching: Report does not disclose the projected value reduction; switch report [P6] clearly shows -£2,000 or -1.6% decrease at mid growth, -£2,000 or -1.9% at low growth, and -£3,000 or -2.0% at high growth.\n6. Charge comparison basis: Report states -0.24% difference but derived figures show this implies a fund value of £103,438.46 (Zurich's value), not the consolidated £110,185 transfer value. The comparison appears to be on a single arrangement, not the consolidated position.","comparisonPerformed":true,"missingInputs":"The report does not disclose the basis on which the -0.24% charge difference was calculated. The derived figures indicate it was calculated on Zurich's fund value (£103,438.46) rather than the consolidated transfer value (£110,185). The report does not explain why a comparison on a single arrangement is presented as the cost of the overall recommendation. The report does not provide the charge breakdown for the recommended Aviva solution that would allow verification of the -0.24% figure. The report does not disclose the RIY calculation methodology or the specific charges included in each RIY figure extracted.","analysis":"The requirement tests whether higher charges producing lower projected outcomes are disclosed and explained. The evidence reveals a fundamental contradiction in the report's cost assertions and a material discrepancy between the report's projections and the supporting switch analysis.\n\nFirst, the cost direction is internally contradictory. The report states the recommendation is 0.24% cheaper but simultaneously states it is 4.24% more expensive (in the required outperformance context). These statements cannot both be true. The 4.24% figure appears to relate to the total cost impact including adviser fees (initial £3,305 plus 1% ongoing), while the -0.24% appears to relate to product and platform charges alone. However, the report does not clarify this distinction, creating ambiguity about whether the recommendation is cheaper or more expensive overall.\n\nSecond, the projected outcomes in the report contradict the switch analysis. The report projects £115,000 at mid growth (2.95%), but the switch report [P6] shows £125,000 for the new solution at the same growth rate. This £10,000 discrepancy is material and unexplained. The switch report [P6] also shows that the new solution projects lower values than the existing arrangement at all three growth rates (low, mid, and high), with reductions of 1.6% to 2.0%. The report does not disclose this reduction in projected outcomes.\n\nThird, the critical yield figures are inconsistent. The report states 4.8% critical yield, but the switch report [P6] shows 3.2% for the new solution at mid growth. The report's figure is 1.6 percentage points higher, suggesting a more demanding outperformance requirement than the evidence supports. The guard states that required outperformance under about 0.5% per year is within noise; 1.6 percentage points is well above this threshold and material.\n\nFourth, the RIY values extracted by the report (0.3%, 2.1%, 0.3%, 2.2%, 0.3%, 2.3%) do not reconcile with the switch report's stated RIY figures (0.3% for existing, 0.6% for new at mid growth). The report extracts six RIY values but does not explain which arrangements or scenarios they relate to, making it impossible to verify their accuracy or reconcile them with the switch analysis.\n\nFifth, the charge comparison basis is unclear. The derived figures show that the -0.24% difference implies a fund value of £103,438.46, which is Zurich's current value, not the consolidated £110,185 transfer value. This suggests the cost comparison was performed on a single arrangement rather than the consolidated position, yet the report presents it as the cost of the overall recommendation. This is a material basis issue that undermines the reliability of the cost comparison.\n\nSixth, the report does not disclose the effect of switching on projected values. The switch report [P6] clearly shows reductions of £2,000 to £3,000 (1.6% to 2.0%) across all growth rates. The report's silence on this reduction, combined with its assertion that the recommendation is cheaper, creates a misleading impression of the value proposition.\n\nThe expected results document [P16] identifies that the switch reduces projected maturity value at every growth rate before advice charges (-1.6% at mid growth) and after them (-9.4%), and notes that the adviser's own ranking places the existing arrangement above the recommendation. This corroborates the finding that higher charges (or at least not lower charges) are producing lower projected outcomes, which is not adequately disclosed or explained in the report.\n\nThe report's statement that \"Based on the past performance of your recommended funds, I do believe that this is achievable\" (page 15) attempts to justify the 4.8% critical yield requirement, but this is weak justification because: (1) past performance is not a guarantee of future returns (as the report itself acknowledges elsewhere); (2) the critical yield of 4.8% is materially higher than the 3.2% shown in the switch report, suggesting the adviser's own analysis does not support this requirement; and (3) the report does not explain why the client should accept lower projected outcomes in exchange for a recommendation that requires higher outperformance to break even.\n\nThe vulnerability overlay is not material here. The client is recorded as having no vulnerability, and the cost and charge issues identified do not appear to be driven by or materially affected by vulnerability characteristics.","citations":[{"passageId":"P6","source":"Pension switch report - updated.md","category":"G","quote":"Projected value at age 75: Existing pension £127,000, New solution £125,000; Effect of switching: -£2,000 or -1.6% decrease; Reduction in yield: Existing 0.3%, New solution 0.6%; Critical yield: 3.2%","cells":null},{"passageId":"P15","source":"Suitability Report Test 1.md","category":"I","quote":"Based on a mid-growth rate of 2.94%, including our advice fees, the recommended plan would need to grow by 4.8% per year up to age 75 in order to meet the same value as your current plan.","cells":null},{"passageId":"P16","source":"expected-results.md","category":null,"quote":"The switch reduces projected maturity value at every growth rate, before advice charges (−1.6% at mid growth) and after them (−9.4%)","cells":null},{"passageId":"P3","source":"Pension switch report - updated.md","category":"G","quote":"Projected value at age 75 (Mid growth): Existing Solution £127,000, New solution £125,000; Effect of switching: -£2,000 or -1.6% decrease","cells":null},{"passageId":"P1","source":"Aviva Policy Info.md","category":"E","quote":"These figures are estimates only and aren't guaranteed. Actual amounts could be more or less and may be worth less than has been paid in.","cells":null}],"issueCategories":["Data inconsistency","Weak justification","Disclosure shortfall"],"severity":"High","outcome":"PotentialConcern"}

