tes \"Currently, your Inheritance Tax liability will be £0.\" The evidence does not establish the client's marginal tax rate, which is material to understanding the tax consequence of income withdrawals and the small pot calculation.","discrepancies":"1. The report estimates £170 net from a £200 small pot withdrawal (25% tax-free = £50, 75% taxable = £150, with tax of £30 implied). This implies a marginal tax rate of 20% (£30 tax on £150 = 20%). However, the report does not establish the client's actual marginal tax rate anywhere in the evidence provided. The client is aged 69, in receipt of state pension (£230/week = £11,960 pa), and earns £300/week net from HGV work plus £20/week volunteer work. The net income figures do not establish gross income or tax band. The calculation is therefore unverifiable against the client's actual circumstances. 2. The report states that pensions are \"currently outside of your estate for IHT purposes\" and \"currently, your Inheritance Tax liability will be £0\", but does not disclose the client's total estate value, property ownership, or spouse's estate, which are material to understanding whether the IHT change from 6 April 2027 will have a material impact on the client's position. The report notes nil rate bands (£325,000 NRB + £175,000 RNRB) but does not quantify the client's exposure. 3. The report states that flexi-access drawdown provides \"a tax-efficient way of passing wealth down through the generations as any unused pension fund can usually be passed down to a wide range of beneficiaries free from Inheritance Tax\" [P10], but this conflicts with the statement that from 6 April 2027 \"most unused defined contribution pensions and death benefits (such as drawdown funds and uncrystallised pots) will be included in your taxable estate for IHT\" [P1]. The first statement implies ongoing IHT efficiency; the second indicates this will change materially. The report does not reconcile these or explain the consequence of the change for the client's planning.","comparisonPerformed":true,"missingInputs":"1. Client's marginal tax rate (gross income not established; net figures provided only). 2. Client's total estate value and spouse's estate value (material to IHT planning and the impact of the 6 April 2027 change). 3. Clarification of whether the client's planned flexi-access drawdown will be subject to the new IHT rules from 6 April 2027 and what the consequence will be for the client's specific circumstances.","analysis":"The report discloses generic tax consequences of pension withdrawal and inheritance tax rules, supported by product literature and terms and conditions. However, three material gaps emerge: (1) The small pot calculation assumes a 20% marginal tax rate but the client's actual tax band is not established in the evidence. The client receives state pension (£11,960 pa) and net employment income (£15,600 pa from HGV work + £1,040 from volunteering = £16,640 net). Without gross income figures, the marginal tax rate cannot be verified. The calculation may be illustrative only, but it is presented as a specific estimate (\"We estimate you would receive £170 net\") without caveat about the client's tax position. (2) The report states current IHT liability is £0 and notes nil rate bands, but does not disclose the client's total estate or property value. The evidence shows £6,000 cash savings and pension arrangements of approximately £110,000, but does not mention property ownership despite the narrative reference to \"your main residence\" in the financial position section. The IHT change from 6 April 2027 is disclosed, but without the client's estate value, the materiality of this change cannot be assessed. (3) The report states that FAD provides \"a tax-efficient way of passing wealth down through the generations as any unused pension fund can usually be passed down to a wide range of beneficiaries free from Inheritance Tax\" [P10], but this is contradicted by the later statement