{"groupId":"G9.3","requirement":"Details of lost features and guarantees — Consistency","reportSays":"The suitability report asserts that all five existing arrangements contain no safeguarded benefits. For each arrangement, it states: \"This pension arrangement does not contain any safeguarded benefits\" (or a variant with a typo: \"This does pension arrangement not contain any safeguarded benefits\"). The report also asserts that no employer contributions are being lost on any of the four replacement analyses (all four values for employerContributionsLost are false). The report does not extract or address any other features or guarantees that may be lost on the arrangements being switched.","fileSays":"The case file contains provider documentation for each existing arrangement. Zurich policy information [P7] confirms the plan holds \"Former protected rights\" with a current value of £66,045.18 (pre-97) and £37,394.06 (post-97), totalling £103,439.24 in former protected rights. The Zurich document states: \"The current transfer value of the former protected rights fund is always the same as the current total fund value.\" [P7] also notes: \"If this plan has capital units, there will be an exit charge applied if the fund is transferred. This exit charge is a standard term of the contract. The charge is taken in order to recover the charges, which would be due, if the fund were to remain invested with Zurich until the customer has reached their selected retirement age (SRA). The charge is taken by reducing the number of capital units paid out; the charge reduces by around 0.3%-0.4% a month until SRA where there is no charge. The charge is capped at 1% from age 55.\" [P3] People's Pension policy information [P6] and [P15] confirm no transfer charges and no restrictions on partial transfers for amounts under £10,500. Scottish Widows [P14] confirms no Guaranteed Annuity Rates, no Guaranteed Minimum Pension, and no Guaranteed Minimum Fund. Aviva [P16] confirms no protected tax-free lump sum and no block/bulk transfers in. Standard Life [P18] confirms the plan can take up to 25% tax-free lump sum with the remainder as taxable income. None of the provider documents confirm the absence of safeguarded benefits; they document specific features, guarantees, and exit terms instead.","discrepancies":"1. The report asserts all five arrangements contain no safeguarded benefits, but provides no provider documentation confirming this assertion. The extraction metadata notes that `/replacementAnalysis[0]/featuresOrGuaranteesLost` is absent from the document (\"NotPresentInDocument\"), indicating the report does not address what features or guarantees are being lost on any of the switched arrangements. 2. The Zurich arrangement (EA4, £103,439.24) contains former protected rights totalling £103,439.24 [P7], which are a form of safeguarded benefit under pension law. The report's assertion that this arrangement \"does not contain any safeguarded benefits\" contradicts the provider documentation. 3. The Zurich policy [P3] and [P7] document an exit charge structure (0.3%-0.4% per month, capped at 1% from age 55) that applies if the fund is transferred. The report does not disclose or analyse this switching cost in the replacement analysis for Zurich. 4. The report asserts no employer contributions are being lost on any arrangement, but EA1 (People's Pension) is a live workplace scheme with ongoing employer contributions of £897.13 annually [extracted model]. The report's advice action for EA1 is \"Retain\" [P22], so this is consistent; however, the report does not explicitly address what would be lost if this arrangement were transferred, despite it being a live scheme with active employer contributions. 5. The report does not extract or disclose any analysis of other valuable features such as death benefits, disability benefits, annuity options, or investment choice restrictions that may differ between existing and recommended arrangements.","compari