tion shows monthly income of £3,486.67 against expenditure of £1,700, with a surplus of £1,786.67 [canonical model]. However, this surplus depends on continued employment income (£1,300 net monthly from HGV driving) which is stated to end \"within the next two years\" (by age 71) [canonical model: `/parties/clients[0]/employments[2]/intendedRetirementDate` = 2027-10-14]. Post-retirement, the household income would be approximately £2,230 (State Pensions £1,000 + Kim's employment £1,100 + volunteer role £86.67), which is insufficient to cover the stated £1,700 expenditure target without pension drawdown. This contradicts the assertion of non-reliance.","comparisonPerformed":true,"missingInputs":"No critical inputs are missing. The vulnerability assessment paths are absent from the extracted model because they are not present in the report document itself, not because extraction failed. The file contains sufficient evidence of the client's knowledge, experience, capacity, and financial circumstances to assess whether vulnerability indicators exist and whether they have been properly considered in the recommendation.","analysis":"The requirement asks whether information relating to client vulnerabilities, support needs, communication preferences, capacity, comprehension requirements or duty of care considerations is fully supported by evidence and applied consistently. The decision logic states: \"No Issue: Evidence complete, consistent and supports the outcome. Vulnerability was assessed and recorded, any characteristics found are reflected in the report's description of support needs, communication and adjustments, and the recommendation accounts for them.\" The finding here is that vulnerability was NOT assessed and recorded, despite clear indicators in the file. The client demonstrates: (1) No understanding of investing, stocks and shares, pension planning, or associated risks [P8, D]; (2) Limited experience to workplace pensions only [P14, I]; (3) A stated knowledge level of \"Moderate\" that contradicts the questionnaire evidence; (4) Recommendation of a Complex product with 6,900+ fund choices and flexible drawdown options, which is misaligned with demonstrated capability; (5) A risk rating override from 4 to 5 without documented justification for why this override is appropriate given the knowledge gap; (6) Financial reliance on pension income post-retirement that is not explicitly acknowledged in the capacity for loss assessment. The FCA guidance FG21/1 on vulnerability identifies low capability and low resilience as vulnerability drivers. This client exhibits low capability (no understanding of investments, limited experience) and faces a life event (retirement within 2 years) with a complex product recommendation. The report's assertion that \"any potential underperformance would not have a detrimental impact on your day-to-day living\" [P14, I] is based on current surplus income, but this surplus is temporary and dependent on continued employment. The overlay requirement asks whether vulnerability has a material impact on the recommendation. Here, the material issue is that: (1) vulnerability indicators exist but were not formally assessed; (2) the recommendation (Complex product, risk level 5, 6,900+ fund choices) appears misaligned with the client's demonstrated knowledge and experience; (3) no support measures, communication adjustments, or comprehension safeguards are documented; (4) the client's post-retirement financial position creates reliance on pension income that is not adequately reflected in the capacity for loss assessment. The guard states: \"Where vulnerability is genuinely absent and that has been assessed and recorded, the overlay adds nothing.\" This is not the case here. Vulnerability was not assessed; it was simply asserted as absent. The evidence shows indicators that should have triggered assessment under FG21/1 (low capability, life event, financial transition). The failure to assess and record vulnerability, combined with a recommendation that appears misaligned with demonstrated capability, constitutes a material compliance concern.","citations":[{"passageId":"P8","source":"Risk profile report.md","category":"D","quote":"When it comes to your experience and understanding of investing, how would you describe yourself? No understanding / knowledge. Do you understand how Current Accounts, Savings/Deposit Accounts, Cash ISA, National Savings and Investments work and do you know the risks associated with them? Very little understanding / knowledge. Do you understand how Unit Trusts and General Investment Accounts work and do you know the risks associated with them? No understanding / knowledge. Do you understand how stocks and shares work and do you know the risks associated with them? No understanding / knowledge. Do you understand how Enterprise Investment Schemes and Venture Capital Trusts work and do you know the risks associated with them? No understanding / knowledge. Do you understand how pension planning and income in retirement works and do you know the risks associated with them? No understanding / knowledge.","cells":null},{"passageId":"P14","source":"Suitability Report Test 1.md","category":"I","quote":"although your experience is limited to your workplace pension arrangements, you understand that returns are not guaranteed","cells":null},{"passageId":"P14","source":"Suitability Report Test 1.md","category":"I","quote":"You completed an Attitude to Risk questionnaire. We felt that this didn't fully reflect your risk appetite. We agreed that you are a risk rating 5 rather than a 4 as per the questionnaire.","cells":null},{"passageId":"P14","source":"Suitability Report Test 1.md","category":"I","quote":"You confirmed that any potential underperformance would not have a detrimental impact on your day-to-day living.","cells":null},{"passageId":"P19","source":"Suitability Report Test 1.md","category":"I","quote":"We have recommended a multi-asset fund because it provides a professionally managed, diversified investment solution that aligns with your financial goals and risk profile. These funds are risk-profiled solutions, meaning they are specifically designed to remain within your agreed level of risk, ensuring your investments stay aligned with your preferences. These funds combine the growth potential of equities with the potentially uncorrelated returns of other asset classes, such as bonds, property and absolute return.","cells":null},{"passageId":"P21","source":"Suitability Report Test 1.md","category":"I","quote":"It would also be prudent to set up and register Power of Attorney for your financial affairs / health and welfare, to ensure that in the event of your incapacity, your spouse or relative could act on your behalf.","cells":null},{"passageId":"P23","source":"Suitability Report Test 1.md","category":"I","quote":"You are currently aged 69 and married to Kim, aged 65. You are both in good health and have three grown-up children, all of whom are financially independent. You work full-time as an HGV driver through an agency, typically earning around £300 per week net, with additional income of £20 per week from your volunteer role with the Sea Cadets. Kim earns approximately £1,100 per month net. You are in receipt of your full State Pension of £230 per week, and Kim will receive her full State Pension of around £230 per week when she reaches State Pension age. You expect to retire within the next two years, while Kim intends to continue working until she reaches 66, at which point she plans to retire fully. Your current household expenditure is approximately £1,700 per month, covering rent, utilities, food, transport, entertainment, and insurance. You hold savings of around £6,000 and have no outstanding liabilities, providing a secure financial base.","cells":null}],"issueCategories":["Missing evidence","Weak justification","Compliance concern"],"severity":"High","outcome":"PotentialConcern"}

